candidates                              : 244
total roots found                       : 766
  of which F_2 roots (0 and 1)          : 242   -> 0.992 per lambda   [null model predicts 1.000]
  of which non-F_2 roots                : 524 -> 2.148 per lambda  [null model predicts ~1]
lambdas carrying a full 131-orbit       : 4   [null model predicts 1.86]
Poisson(mean 1.86) P[>= 4 such lambdas]    : 0.119

The involution lambda(X) -> lambda(X+1)+1 pairs them, so the number of
INDEPENDENT events is 2, against an expected 244/(2*131) = 0.93 pairs;
Poisson(mean 0.93) P[>= 2 pairs] = 0.239

CONCLUSION (stated as a control, not as a finding):
  the observed maximum is exactly 1 + 131 = one F_2 root plus ONE Frobenius
  orbit, which is the smallest non-trivial value the orbit structure permits
  above 2, and the number of lambdas reaching it is consistent with the null
  model at ordinary significance.  Nothing in this computation is evidence that
  the four maximizing lambdas are algebraically special; they are the lambdas
  that happened to pick up one orbit.  A claim that they are special would need
  its own evidence, which this task neither sought nor produced.

Sanity check on the null model at small n, where 2^n' can be SMALLER than n:
  n=11, n'=3: deg L = max(2^3, d) = 8 < 11, so a non-F_2 root would need 11
  conjugates inside a degree-8 polynomial -- impossible.  The n=11 sweep indeed
  found NO non-F_2 roots at all (histogram {0:62, 1:124, 2:62}), which is a
  prediction of the structure, not an accident.  For n=131, n'=33, deg L = 2^33
  >> 131, so the obstruction is absent and orbits are possible -- as observed.
